    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bb87e80c_531b0019",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/d3d11/BufferD3D11.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 1481,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-17T06:22:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would prefer not bloating the code with abundant `CHECK`. I think we should be consistent with the principle \"don\u0027t use check in tight loops\" even if it\u0027s cheap. We don\u0027t want future devs to see this and assume it\u0027s required to do CHECK everywhere.\n\nI already see we are adding at least 8 `CHECK` to a hot path (4 here, 1 in IncrStorageRevAndMakeLatest, 3 in BindGroupTracker). That\u0027s roughly 16 instructions. `IsAlign()` and `||` are more than 2 instructions anw.\n\nLet\u0027s say we have 1 millions draw calls per frame. That\u0027s translated to extra 16000000 instructions. Assuming the CPU freq is 3Ghz. And each instruction is 1 cycle:\n- 16000000 / 3000000000 \u003d extra 5.3ms per frame.\n\nWhen analysing real-world data, 99th percentile is very difficult to reproduce and investigate. So I would like to avoid adding extra complexities for now. FWIW, D3D11 is not exposed to WebGPU clients yet.",
      "parentUuid": "f497b326_be34ec18",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1481,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1481,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "1b216014a2cacc40b6735500ca4afe657c558685",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5130743a_5e27acfb",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/d3d11/BufferD3D11.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 1481,
      "author": {
        "id": 3461520
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-17T13:43:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "First lets look at the analysis. \nA check is usually a cmp and jmp. The jmps only go through the front end if they are not taken so really the you only have a single extra instruction.\n\nSecond our IPC is very low in general. Independent instructions like these side checks will disappear in the existing instruction bubbles. \n\nBut coming back to the computation. How many instructions do you think a single draw call is; going from javascript all the way down to the hardware? Probably hundreds at a minimum. Also I imagine one changing something other that just issuing the same draw call (aka bindgroups etc). I dont think that you can do 1 million draw calls at even close to 60fps on WebGPU on any hardware. Which means this ballpark figure (5.3ms) should not be put in the context of a 16ms frame. \n\nHowever at a high level, I totally agree that checks , anywhere, should not be necessary and suggest a defensive coding style. They suggest something is wrong!\n\nThe problem is that something is indeed wrong. \n\nI dont mind removing all the \u0027hot\u0027 code checks from this CL. But I think we should find all the asserts that wont induce heavy perf and convert them to checks. We will then get reports when these fail and prevent future security issues that would have triggered asserts.",
      "parentUuid": "bb87e80c_531b0019",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1481,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1481,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "1b216014a2cacc40b6735500ca4afe657c558685",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6175881f_3158519f",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/d3d11/BufferD3D11.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 1481,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-20T11:58:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK, You got some points. This might be largely cheap in general.\n\nHowever, D3D11 is only used by Graphite in the GPU process and never goes through javascript things. Its timing could be affected more than WebGPU.\n\nAre we planning to review these checks in future and remove unnecessary ones (after getting no report of security failures)?",
      "parentUuid": "5130743a_5e27acfb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1481,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1481,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "1b216014a2cacc40b6735500ca4afe657c558685",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bd04e6d8_16082028",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/d3d11/BufferD3D11.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 1481,
      "author": {
        "id": 3461520
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-20T13:50:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As mentioned in the doc is it hard to remove them but likely we will use AI to help remove them as AI might have been used in finding them.\n\nIf d3d11 will never be access by javascript (I though it was going to be via compat like gles) then we can remove most of these checks in that backend.\n\nAny other places that are hot these checks should be evaluated and removed.\n\nIf you could just do one more pass then we can be confident.",
      "parentUuid": "6175881f_3158519f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1481,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1481,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "1b216014a2cacc40b6735500ca4afe657c558685",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3ddc2490_5a72af4d",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/d3d11/BufferD3D11.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 1481,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-21T01:15:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We might release a D3D11 backend eventually, but I don\u0027t think we actively have plans to do it. It\u0027s going to depend on what the Windows ecosystem looks like by the time we have time to consider it again.\n\nI don\u0027t think we should worry about the total performance that much on D3D11 any more than other backends. Sure they\u0027re older devices, but they\u0027re still desktop, and even older underpowered laptops have pretty fast CPUs (in my experience usually more bottlenecked on RAM, iGPU, disk).\n\nIn any case, security is more important than speed and I think that even though very few bugs will be prevented by this change, it most likely *will* prevent *some* bugs and that will be worth the speed cost.\n\nAlso, there are better ways to approach this than to try to guess the performance of every DAWN_CHECK. I have filed https://crbug.com/504697276 to do systematic benchmarking. However it\u0027s inevitably going to have to wait a bit until security hardening work has settled down.",
      "parentUuid": "bd04e6d8_16082028",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1481,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1481,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "1b216014a2cacc40b6735500ca4afe657c558685",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
